---
import ArticleLayout from '../../components/ArticleLayout.astro'
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro'
---

<ArticleLayout>
    <ol>
        <a href="/blog/aws-getting-started-deploy-static-website-s3"
            ><li>Deploying a Static Website to S3</li></a
        >
        <a href="/blog/aws-getting-started-cloudfront-and-domain"
            ><li>Distribute an S3 Bucket with CloudFront and add a Domain</li></a
        >
        <a href="/blog/aws-getting-started-micro-service-lambda-dynamodb"
            ><li>Micro Services using Lambda, DynamoDB & API Gateway</li></a
        >
    </ol>

    <p>
        In this part, we're going to add CloudFront to deliver our website content with low latency
        to the user. Afterward, we'll add a domain with HTTPs for our website.
    </p>

    <p>
        So let's jump right into it.<br />
        Go to the AWS Management Console and open the service <code class="ui-code">CloudFront</code
        >. Then Click on <code class="ui-code">Create Distribution</code>
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/1-cloudfront-service.jpg"
        alt="cloudfront service"
        width="1600"
        height="890"
    />
    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/2-create-distribution.jpg"
        alt="cloudfront create distribution"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        Now click <code class="ui-code">Get Started</code> under the delivery method <code
            class="ui-code">Web</code
        >
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/3-get-started.jpg"
        alt="cloudfront get started"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        On the Origin Settings, the field <code class="ui-code">Origin Domain Name</code> is already pre-filled
        with your S3 Bucket as an option. But as we already added <code class="ui-code"
            >Static website hosting</code
        > to our bucket, we don't want to use the direct link to our bucket in this field. Instead, we
        have to provide the URL of our bucket.
    </p>
    <p>
        So open a new tab, go to the S3 service and open your bucket. Then copy the URL from the
        bucket properties and paste it into <code class="ui-code">Origin Domain Name</code>.
    </p>

    <img src="/blog/aws2/4-bucket-url.jpg" alt="s3 bucket url" width="1600" height="887" />
    <img src="/blog/aws2/5-cloudfront-url.jpg" alt="set cloudfront url" width="1600" height="887" />

    <p>
        Most of the fields should be already filled now.<br />
        Scroll down and enable <code class="ui-code">Compress Objects Automatically</code>. This
        will enable gzip compression for your content.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/6-cloudfront-compress.jpg"
        alt="set cloudfront gzip compression"
        width="1600"
        height="401"
    />

    <p>
        Then add the <code class="ui-code">Default Root Object</code>. This is the entry point of
        your website. For me that is <code class="ui-code">index.html</code>. Afterward, you can
        click <code class="ui-code">Create Distribution</code>.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/7-cloudfront-create.jpg"
        alt="create cloudfront"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        It will take a moment until your distribution is created. When it's done, you can open the
        domain, which you can see in the list.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/8-cloudfront-url.jpg"
        alt="url of cloudfront distribution"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        For more details on your CloudFront distribution, you can click the ID. You can also set the
        error page there if you want to.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/9-cloudfront-details.jpg"
        alt="cloudfront details"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        If you now deploy to your bucket again, you'll notice, that the content won't change. This
        is because CloudFront is caching your content. To invalidate the cache you have to run
        following command in your console:
    </p>

    <CodeBlock
        value="aws cloudfront create-invalidation --distribution-id=YOUR_DISTRIBUTION_ID --paths /"
    />

    <p>
        Where you have to replace <code class="ui-code">YOUR_DISTRIBUTION_ID</code> with the ID of your
        CloudFront distribution.
    </p>

    <h2>Add a domain with HTTPs</h2>

    <p>
        If you don't have a domain yet, you can buy one on the AWS service <code class="ui-code"
            >Route 53</code
        >. Otherwise you can skip this step. Don't worry if your domain is sitting somewhere else
        then Route 53. I'll also go through the process for that as well.
    </p>

    <img src="/blog/aws2/10-route53-service.jpg" alt="route 53 service" width="1600" height="271" />
    <br />
    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/11-domain-registration.jpg"
        alt="route 53 domain registration"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        I won't go into detail about the purchase process. Choose a domain name and then follow the
        steps.
    </p>
    <p>Next, go to the certificate manager to get an SSL certificate for your website.</p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/12-certificate-service.jpg"
        alt="certificate service"
        width="1600"
        height="271"
    />
    <br />
    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/13-certificate-get-started.jpg"
        alt="certificate get started page"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        Before clicking <code class="ui-code">Request public certificate</code>, make sure you
        switch regions to <code class="ui-code">us-east-1</code>. This is necessary, because
        CloudFront only supports the US East region for certificates.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/14-certificate-region.jpg"
        alt="certificate switch region"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        Then continue and enter the domain you want to use on the next page. You can also use
        multiple ones like
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><code class="ui-code">wweb.dev</code></li>
        <li><code class="ui-code">*.wweb.dev</code></li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        With this, you will secure the main domain, as well as <code class="ui-code"
            >www.wweb.dev</code
        > and all sub-domains. <br />
        I will go for only adding a certificate for the specific sub-domain <code class="ui-code"
            >aws.wweb.dev</code
        >
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/15-certificate-domain.jpg"
        alt="certificate choose domain"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        Afterward, I'll go for DNS validation. This has some advantages over Email validation, like
        automatic renewal of the certificates.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/16-dns-validation.jpg"
        alt="certificate dns validation"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        Click <code class="ui-code">Next</code>. We can skip setting tags, so click <code
            class="ui-code">Review</code
        > on the next page. On the following page, you can click <code class="ui-code"
            >Confirm and request</code
        > if the data you see there is correct.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/17-confirm.jpg"
        alt="certificate confirm screen"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        On the next page, expand your domain by clicking that small arrow next to it. Here we have
        two options, depending on your domain being hosted on Route53 or not.
    </p>

    <p>
        <b>Option 1)</b> If your domain is hosted on Route 53 you will see a button, saying <code
            class="ui-code">Create record in Route 53</code
        >. Click that button. Then in the modal, hit <code class="ui-code">Create</code>.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/18-route53-dns.jpg"
        alt="certificate for route53"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />
    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/19-route53-dns-2.jpg"
        alt="certificate for route53 modal"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        <b>Option 2)</b> If you have your domain somewhere else it will look like this:
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/20-cname-details.jpg"
        alt="certificate with cname validation"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        Now you have to go to your domain provider and open the DNS settings. There you have to
        create a new CNAME record for your domain.
    </p>
    <p>
        How you create the CNAME record might differ for different providers. I had to remove the
        main domain from the host (<code class="ui-code">.wweb.dev</code>) as this is appended
        automatically by Namecheap (where I have my domain).
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/21-namecheap.jpg"
        alt="adding CNAME to namecheap"
        width="1600"
        height="211"
    />

    <p>
        You have to do this for all the domains you've added.<br />
        Now, wait until the status of your certificate turned to <code class="ui-code">Issued</code
        >. This can take 5-10 minutes.
    </p>

    <img src="/blog/aws2/22-verified.jpg" alt="issued certificate" width="1600" height="887" />

    <h2>Add your domain to CloudFront</h2>

    <p>
        Go back to your CloudFront distribution. Go into the distribution details by clicking the ID
        and then click the <code class="ui-code">Edit</code> button.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/23-cloudfront-details.jpg"
        alt="cloudfront details"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />
    <img src="/blog/aws2/24-cloudfront-edit.jpg" alt="cloudfront edit" width="1600" height="887" />

    <p>
        Add the domain to <code class="ui-code">Alternate Domain Names</code>. Then click on <code
            class="ui-code">Custom SSL Certificate</code
        > and select your new certificate from the dropdown.<br />
        Afterward, scroll down and hit <code class="ui-code">Yes, Edit</code>.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/25-cloudfront-add-url.jpg"
        alt="cloudfront add ssl and url"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        Now let's create a redirect from HTTP to HTTPS. Therefor go to <code class="ui-code"
            >Behaviours</code
        >, select the behavior on top and click <code class="ui-code">Edit</code>.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/26-cloudfront-behaviour.jpg"
        alt="cloudfront edit behaviour"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        In the edit view, select <code class="ui-code">Redirect HTTP to HTTPS</code> and then again scroll
        down and click <code class="ui-code">Yes, Edit</code>.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/27-cloudfront-redirect.jpg"
        alt="cloudfront add redirect"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        Now as the last step we need to point our domain to CloudFront. Here we have two possible
        options again. You either have your domain on Route 53 or you have it hosted somewhere else.
    </p>

    <h3>Option 1) Route 53</h3>
    <p>
        Go back to the <code class="ui-code">Route 53</code> service. Then select <code
            class="ui-code">Hosted zones</code
        > in the left menu and click on your domain.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/28-route53-hosted-zone.jpg"
        alt="route 53 hosted zone"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        Now click <code class="ui-code">Create Record Set</code>. A menu on the right will open. You
        can leave the <code class="ui-code">Name</code> blank if you want to use your main URL. You can
        also use www or a subdomain in that field.
    </p>

    <p>
        Select <code class="ui-code">Yes</code> for Alias. Then select your CloudFront distribution in
        the menu for the <code class="ui-code">Alias Target</code>.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/29-route53-record.jpg"
        alt="route 53 add record"
        width="1600"
        height="887"
    />

    <p>
        You can add IPv6 support by creating another record with the same settings, but with <code
            class="ui-code">AAAA - IPv6 address</code
        > as the type.
    </p>

    <h3>Option 2) Other Providers</h3>

    <p>
        For any other provider, you have to create another CNAME DNS entry. This one should point
        from your domain to the CloudFront URL. For example, on Namecheap, it would look like this
        for my <code class="ui-code">aws</code> subdomain.
    </p>

    <img
        src="/blog/aws2/30-namecheap-cname.jpg"
        alt="namecheap cloudfront CNAME"
        width="2278"
        height="288"
    />

    <p>
        For both options, it may take up to an hour until the new DNS is applied.<br />
        Then your Domain should be set up correctly and point to your CloudFront.
    </p>

    <p>
        In the next and last part of this series, I'll show you how to create a microservice with
        AWS Lambda, DynamoDB, and the API Gateway.
    </p>

    <p>
        <a href="/blog/aws-getting-started-micro-service-lambda-dynamodb"
            >Getting Started with Serverless AWS (3/3) - Distribute an S3 Bucket with CloudFront and
            add a Domain</a
        >
    </p>

    <p>
        Did you had problems following along at some point? Please let me know, so I can improve
        this tutorial :)
    </p>
</ArticleLayout>
